WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:06.540
Every form of life on our planet — from
species that swim in the water to those that

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.760
soar through the air, from the largest land
mammals to the smallest of insects — all

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.449
these creatures and their patterns of life
are, in some way or another, determined by

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:19.869
the same, invisible force: gravity.

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:25.759
Earth’s gravitational pull influences the
parameters of animals’ body plans, maximum

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:30.550
size, and behavior — often limiting the
ways in which life can evolve.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.380
But an earth-like level of gravity isn’t
a constant through the universe.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.360
Imagine a planet where gravity is much, much
weaker than it is on earth.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.370
What kinds of fantastical forms of alien life
might evolve?

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:49.390
This speculative hypothetical is the inspiration
behind the fictional alien world of Anu, a

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:52.400
creation of the talented artist Sam Vilasboas.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.710
The organisms on Anu have evolved into very
different forms than what could feasibly exist

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:58.829
on our own planet.

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:04.420
Vilasboas is an awesome worldbuilder, often
creating entire diagrams going into the hypothetical

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:09.310
science behind the forms of low-gravity life
populating planet Anu — including an entire

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:14.249
taxonomy chart showing how the various aliens
we’ll be exploring in this video are related

00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:15.280
to each other.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.270
If you want to check out more of their work,
as usual I have links to them in this video’s

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:20.270
description.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:25.369
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
be taking a documentary-style deep-dive into

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:29.060
this incredibly detailed low-gravity world.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:33.939
Not surprisingly, the most fantastical forms
are in the air, but let’s start where life

00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:36.520
often begins — under the waves.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.700
The small, rocky planet of Anu doesn’t have
much water compared to earth.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:45.969
But like on our own planet, multicellular
life likely began within these depths.

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:51.609
Starting at the base of the Anu evolutionary-tree,
we have the very simple Necroviscera, jellyfish

00:01:51.609 --> 00:01:56.340
like organisms with emaciated forms that swim
in the low-pressure depths.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.210
These lightweight organisms are small and
relatively uncomplicated, but they’re basal

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:04.890
to many other more fantastical lifeforms on
the planet.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:09.510
Moving up the evolutionary tree, we have these
“fish-like” lifeforms present in most

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:11.390
bodies of water on Anu.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.140
Ingentriadix are a basic example of such an
organism.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:20.090
Thanks to the semi-universal nature of hydro
dynamics, the artist imagines these lifeforms

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.920
aren’t much different from fish on our own
planet — although they don’t have to worry

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.580
as much about water pressure in the depths
thanks to the lower gravity.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:33.030
Feeding on organisms like Ingentriadix are
species like the Litzacrista, early predators

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.000
about the size of a tiger shark.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.700
They usually hunt alone, ensnaring prey in
their four-part jaws — which the artist

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.080
imagines most species on planet Anu have due
to a common ancestor.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.280
This is another species which lives in the
low-pressure depths, drifting along on silent

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.460
fins.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:57.030
On land, organisms become more complicated,
and start to truly adopt unfamiliar forms

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.390
which take advantage of the low gravity.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:04.510
This spindly group is the Flammanasus — organisms
that likely wouldn’t be able to stand up

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.040
on our planet, but move just fine in Anu’s
gravity.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.540
The artist imagines they travel in packs,
like you can see here along one of Anu’s

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:13.540
many beaches.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.570
In this image, a young Flammanasus picks a
senseless fight with another pack member to

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:18.570
show strength.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.570
And according to the artist, loses quite badly.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:26.560
In another image, two Flammanasus walk along
the muddy bank of a river, looking for a high

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.690
spot to warm themselves in the sun.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:32.010
Temperature regulation is critical to Flammanasus
survival.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:36.950
The artist imagines that specialized organs
all along their bodies accumulate and burn

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:39.080
fats, creating heat when needed.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:44.100
The Flammanasus use this adaptation in a specialized
manner: they can heat the air stored in the

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:45.870
space in their upper jaw.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:50.280
At night, Flammanasus — which can see in
infrared light — use these flashes of heat

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.380
to communicate with one another, as this image
demonstrates.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:04:00.100
A larger land-dwelling organism is the Dracocentaurus
— a species with legs too spindly to support

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:05.270
a sizable body on our planet, but whose limbs
are effective in its own low-gravity environment.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:10.260
These omnivorous creatures have evolved two
long, pointed limbs for spearing prey, which

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.320
superficially resembles the insectoid claws
on various types of mantises.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:20.320
Despite their fearsome appearance, the large
Dracocentaurus are a social species with some

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.650
signs of early intelligence, with packs exhibiting
significant social complexity.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:30.910
Even larger still, and less cunning, are the
gargantuan Brachicornibus.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:36.060
These mighty grazers take full advantage of
the low gravity, growing over three times

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.840
the size of an earth elephant.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:43.230
Their strange, antler like front limbs are
a powerful form of defense, making them essentially

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.230
untouchable.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:47.820
But the Brachicornibus aren’t the largest
organisms on planet Anu.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.810
That distinction goes to the Necrocampus Continentale
— which at a glance just look like a field

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.990
of savanna grasses, but are actually a single
massive lifeform.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:57.410
A cross-section reveals these ‘grasses’
are just the top part of the huge, cell-like

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:00.630
structures that makes up these curious organisms.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:05.461
The artist imagines some of these cell networks
form whole biomes of their own, and are so

00:05:05.461 --> 00:05:07.740
large they can be seen from space.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:12.870
In the places where two of these lifeforms
meet, elevated areas form — which makes

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:16.490
the organisms almost analogous to living tectonics
plates.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:19.850
A genuinely unique speculative concept.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:24.310
But the land isn’t the only place to take
advantage of Anu’s low gravity.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:29.740
In the vast skies above this small planet,
a truly unique ecosystem has formed.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:35.800
The skies of Anu are like an aerial ocean,
with lightweight organisms gliding along on

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.260
fins similar to how creatures move through
the seas on Earth.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:45.580
Near the bottom of this aerial oceanic food-chain
are the Endocrura, lightweight organisms whose

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:49.300
size allows them to be easily scattered by
the winds.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:54.370
Most live within the equatorial forests on
Anu, and can display mesmerizing colors like

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:56.800
tropical birds on our own planet.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:02.140
Other Endocrura have adapted to the cold lands
north of the equator, like this fuzzy, sleek

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:06.050
species whose color helps it blend in with
the snow.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:10.370
Often called ‘wolves’ due to their pack
hunting behavior, these species can both leap

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:14.811
great distances to catch prey and scavenge
on the ground — although they aren’t true

00:06:14.811 --> 00:06:17.060
fliers like their relatives.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:20.710
Higher up in the atmosphere, larger organisms
take flight.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:26.250
This is an example of a Foliopalum, a brightly
colored herbivore that sails along on three

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:28.620
sets of paddle-like wings.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:33.500
These colorful noodles eat leaves and giant
spores which are sometimes released into the

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:35.460
air by specific plants.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:40.699
The idea of flying spores reminds me of dandelion
seeds, which, as I’m sure you know, can

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:43.290
also become airborne on gusts of wind.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:46.020
But where there’s herbivores, there are
carnivores.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:52.270
The Enodoptera are a group of large equatorial
predators which the artist has nicknamed the

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:53.270
‘air-sharks.’

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:58.740
One specific species is the Orbualus — a
high-altitude ambush predator that specializes

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.300
in capturing low flying prey.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:06.470
Their keen eyes allow them to scan for prey
far below them, then dive down at high speeds

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.150
and pierce their target with their front limbs.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:14.260
Like birds on earth, Enodoptera will sometimes
rest on the trunks of tall vegetation in between

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:17.610
periods of searching for food, which can be
a draining ordeal.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:22.790
Here, a group takes a break after an unsuccessful
hunt, looking for some shade where they might

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:24.660
hopefully get some rest.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:29.540
Another type of Enodoptera are the Stellapulmo
— snake-like predators that fly along the

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:34.510
riverbanks of a region called Eden Valley
— a biome surrounding the largest lakes

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:39.150
on the planet Anu, and one with the highest
concentration of biomass of any region.

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:44.920
In this lush environment, serpentine Stellapulmo,
flourish in large numbers: catching small

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:47.430
creatures when they spot them from above.

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:53.669
A larger Enodoptera are the Bia-lavis-qualus,
lion-sized aerial predators that glide in

00:07:53.669 --> 00:07:56.710
packs through the skies above mountainous
regions.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:00.639
They hunt and eat any terrestrial animals
on the mountain ranges that they can take

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:05.610
down, which likely includes most species of
the aformentioned Flammanasus.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:11.080
But there are some organisms in the skies
far too large for most predators to consider

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:12.080
hunting.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:16.210
The Abzulong — which actually fall into
the Enodoptera classification despite being

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:21.530
herbivores — have long, narrow bodies which
wouldn’t be able to take to the air in earth’s

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:22.530
gravity.

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:25.949
On Anu, however, the Abzulong rule these clear
skies.

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:28.060
Or at least, mostly.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:32.430
In truth, even the massive Abzulong aren’t
completely safe.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:37.419
Not when the Crommus Rex is on the prowl:
the biggest flying creature in the skies of

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:38.419
Anu.

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:43.520
These giant organisms almost resemble small
airplanes — and consume anything they can

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:44.960
fit in their mouths.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:50.500
Which, as this diagram shows is quite a lot
— as the artist imagines the Crommus Rex

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:55.230
has not a four, but a six-part jaw that can
fit around almost anything.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:59.750
A true apex of the low-gravity ocean-like
skies.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:04.870
The unique low-gravity lifeforms of planet
Anu are just one speculative interpretation

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:09.360
of what life on a planet with lower gravity
than our own might look like.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.820
If you like exploring this fictional world,
consider supporting Sam Vilasboas by visiting

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:15.820
the links below.

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:20.750
There are other species of Anu on their page
which I didn’t get to here, so check those

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:22.690
out if you’re interested.

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:24.600
Also, thanks for watching.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:29.120
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support — and like, subscribe, and hit the

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:43.430
notification icon to stay up to date on all
things Curious.

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:44.580
See you in the next video.

